---
title: Panang Curry
servings: 6
time required: 1 hour
cuisine: Thai
image: https://hot-thai-kitchen.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/panang-pork-sprinkle-4x5-vert.jpg
source: https://hot-thai-kitchen.com/panang-curry/
---

Remember to start your @jasmine rice{} before beginning.

> If you are confident in your cooking ability, you can caramelize palm sugar here, but it is very easy to burn things as it requires high heat. For this simplified recipe, we omit that step here. 

Add @coconut milk{1.25%cup}(Aroy-D carton preferred, pouring at a slight angle to get the creamier part) to a #wok and heat on medium-high heat until thick. Your goal is to lightly caramelize the milk.

> If you're using canned unsweetened coconut milk, use two 14oz cans. Do not shake them. For this step, simply skim the top half of each can for the creamier part.

Add @panang curry paste{1%4 oz can}(Maesri preferred) and @coconut cream{}(Coco Reàl preferred) and fry off a bit.

Add the remaining @coconut milk{1.25%cup} and @palm sugar{110g}(chopped). Mix to combine.

Add @fish sauce{4.5%tbsp}, @peanut butter{2%tbsp}, and @?lime juice{1%tbsp}. Mix to combine.

> You can replace the fish sauce in a pinch or to avoid fish allergies by using 3 tbsp soy sauce and 1.5 tbsp rice vinegar.

Add @bell peppers{2}(cut into strips), @onion{1}(diced), and @chicken breast{1.3%lbs}(sliced). Mix to combine.

Cover and simmer until cooked, about ~{15%minutes} total. At the 10 minute mark, add @?thai sweet basil{1%tbsp}(specifically Thai sweet basil, not holy basil or other types) and @makrut/kaffir lime Leaves{1.5%tsp}(about 2 whole dried makrut lime leaves).

> Be careful to not overcook the lime leaves, as over cooking them removes the citrus flavor. Undercooking, of course, misses out on this flavor.

Serve on top of your jasmine rice.

> If you prefer a thicker panang, make a slurry of 1-2 tbsp cornstarch and mix with a little cold water. Add this to your panang curry.

> Some additional resources from r/food: https://www.reddit.com/r/food/comments/f3as4/comment/c1czjg9 and https://www.reddit.com/r/food/comments/f3as4/comment/m1z4mqv 